# What the build context is NOT.
#
# `COPY system_files/ /` copies from the build context, and the build context is
# this working tree — not what git tracks. `.gitignore` has no say in it. So a
# local build shipped whatever the tree happened to be carrying, while CI (which
# builds from a fresh clone) shipped only what was committed: two images from one
# commit, and the difference invisible until someone looked inside.
#
# Proven, not feared: `podman run localhost/moos ls /usr/bin/__pycache__` listed
# `moai-control.cpython-313.pyc` and friends. An operating system does not ship
# the bytecode cache of the machine that built it — it is dead weight at best, and
# at worst it is *stale* bytecode sitting next to the source it no longer matches.

# Python's bytecode cache — the one that actually shipped.
#
# `**/` is not decoration. These patterns are matched against paths relative to
# the context root, so a bare `__pycache__/` excludes a `__pycache__` sitting in
# THIS directory and nothing else — and the one that shipped was four levels down,
# in `system_files/usr/bin/`. Written without the `**/`, this file looks correct,
# reads correct, and excludes nothing. The build gate caught it.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo

# Editor and OS litter.
.git/
.gitignore
**/*.swp
**/*~
**/.DS_Store

# Build output of the vendored apps. MoPlayer and Mo Remote are compiled *inside*
# the image by their own Containerfile stages; their local build directories are
# 40 MB of binaries the image must never see. (`just sync-moplayer` already vendors
# only what git tracks, but the raw directories are here for anyone who copies one
# in by hand.)
moplayer/build/
moplayer/.dart_tool/
moplayer/linux/flutter/ephemeral/
moremote/bin/
moremote/obj/

# Local build artefacts and scratch.
output/
*.qcow2
*.iso
